#
# racoon.conf - Default Configuration
#
# This is the file `make install` places at this path -- but only if no
# racoon.conf already existed here.  If you are looking at this file after
# an upgrade and expected to see your own edits, they are still in effect;
# this default is never installed over an existing configuration.
#
# It is intentionally role-agnostic and inert: no "remote" or "sainfo"
# block is defined, so racoon starts and idles at near-zero footprint
# with nothing to negotiate and nothing to misconfigure.  Installing this
# package does not, by itself, turn the machine into a VPN client or a
# VPN gateway -- you decide that by choosing and adapting one of the
# example configurations below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which example should I start from?
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# racoon's role (client vs. gateway) is entirely a property of what you put
# in racoon.conf, not something the package chooses for you.  Full,
# heavily-commented worked examples for both roles -- and for several
# narrower features -- are installed under:
#
#   @RACOON_EXAMPLESDIR@/
#
#   examples/roadwarrior/client/racoon.conf
#     Connecting to somebody else's VPN gateway as a roadwarrior client:
#     X.509 certificate + XAuth username/password authentication, Mode
#     Config for a dynamically assigned address, DNS, and split routing.
#     This is the common case if you were handed a certificate and a
#     gateway address by a VPN administrator.  Pairs with
#     examples/roadwarrior/client/phase1-up.sh and phase1-down.sh
#     (historical, minimal reference hooks -- for a maintained split-DNS
#     and routing hook set see racoon-hook-lib.sh(8) / racoon-dns-detect(8)
#     and @RACOON_SYSCONFDIR@/scripts, installed unconditionally by this
#     package as a real component, not an example).
#
#   examples/roadwarrior/server/racoon.conf
#     Running the VPN gateway side of the same roadwarrior setup: address
#     pool, split-DNS, XAuth authentication source (system/PAM/LDAP), and
#     the Mode Config attributes the client example above expects.
#
#   examples/racoon.conf.sample-natt
#     NAT-Traversal (RFC 3947) specifics: listen/timer directives for
#     UDP 4500 port-floating and NAT keepalives.
#
#   examples/racoon.conf.sample-plainrsa
#     PlainRSA authentication (raw RSA keypairs, no X.509 certificates) --
#     useful when interoperating with, or migrating from, a *Swan-family
#     IKEv1 stack.
#
#   examples/racoon.conf.sample-gssapi
#     GSS-API (Kerberos) authentication.
#
#   examples/racoon.conf.sample-inherit
#     "remote ... inherit ..." -- factoring shared proposal/identity
#     settings out of multiple similar remote peers.
#
#   examples/psk.txt.example
#     Format of the pre_shared_key file referenced below.
#
# Each example is self-contained and documents its own prerequisites; none
# of them assume you have already read the others.  Copy the one closest
# to what you need to @RACOON_SYSCONFDIR@/racoon.conf, adapt the addresses,
# identities, and proposal to your environment, then validate before
# reloading:
#
#   racoon -f @RACOON_SYSCONFDIR@/racoon.conf -t
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reference
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   racoon.conf(5), racoon(8), racoon-dns-detect(8), racoonctl(8), setkey(8)
#   racoon-admin-guide.html - full administrator's guide (building,
#     authentication methods, LDAP, Apple client interop, security
#     recommendations, troubleshooting) -- see docs/admin-guide/ in the
#     project source; packaged separately by some distributions.
#   @RACOON_EXAMPLESDIR@/ - every example shipped with the source.

# Log level: "notify" is a reasonable default for a freshly installed,
# not-yet-configured daemon.  Raise to "debug"/"debug2" (or run
# `racoon -d`/`-ddd`) while working out a new configuration.
log notify;

# Pre-shared key file, used by the pre_shared_key/xauth_* authentication
# methods.  Not read until a "remote" block actually needs it, so it is
# safe to leave unset even though the file does not exist yet.
path pre_shared_key "@RACOON_SYSCONFDIR@/psk.txt";

# Directory racoon searches for certificate/key files named by
# certificate_type/peers_certfile/ca_type in a "remote" block.
path certificate "@RACOON_SYSCONFDIR@/certs";
